pg:SectionTree a ipo:TreeNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Section tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "The section tree lists the sections of the chapter by their three levels, and choosing one opens that single section."@en .

pg:Sidebar a ipo:Sidebar,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter explorer"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "The explorer at the left shows the chapter's sections as a tree and keeps pace with the tabs of the main area."@en .

pg:SourceDocument a ipo:SourceDocument,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter 8 Stage 3 document"@en ;
    ipo:hasLearningObjective pg:CO1,
        pg:CO2,
        pg:CO3,
        pg:CO4,
        pg:CO5,
        pg:CO6 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 8 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain why naive key aggregation fails (the key cancellation attack), what MuSig2 changes, and how Shamir secret sharing gives any two of three shares the secret and one share nothing"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain why naive key aggregation fails (the key cancellation attack), what MuSig2 changes, and how Shamir secret sharing gives any two of three shares the secret and one share nothing."@en .

pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Each of the n inputs of a legacy transaction needs a signature check, and each check hashes a copy of the transaction that is about n inputs long, so the total work is about n squared."@en .

pg:Q11_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says the hash operations of the legacy algorithm increase 'roughly quadratically' and those of BIP143 'in O(n)'. The two growth factors from 100 to 1,000 inputs are [97.8, 10.0]: ten times the inputs cost about ..."@en .

pg:Q12_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A BIP, a Bitcoin Improvement Proposal, has a status that says how far it has gone."@en .

pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A digital signature is checked by a verification function that takes three inputs: the message that was signed (in Bitcoin, a hash of a chosen part of the transaction), the public key of the signer, and the signature itself, ..."@en .

pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A SIGHASH flag is a single byte appended to a signature that says which parts of the transaction the signature covers."@en .

pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The reference for what the network accepts is the code of Bitcoin Core, so the chapter reads the constants from the saved copy of src/script/interpreter.h at the pinned commit: SIGHASH_ALL = 1, SIGHASH_NONE = 2, SIGHASH_SINGLE ..."@en .

pg:Q4_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book teaches the schnorr protocol first with whole numbers in place of curve points."@en .

pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The interactive protocol has three moves: the prover sends a commitment, the public nonce kG; the verifier answers with a random challenge e; the prover replies with s = k + e x modulo n."@en .

pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "ECDSA, the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm, produces a signature of two numbers, R and s."@en .

pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A signature scheme is malleable if someone other than the signer can turn a valid signature into a different valid signature for the same message."@en .

pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The nonce k is a one-time secret, and the book's warning is that using it twice destroys the key."@en .

pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "To show that the repetition is what the attack uses, the chapter applies the same recovery formula to two signatures that use different nonces and finds that the number it returns is not the private key, the answer False for ..."@en .

pg:S_ActivationGap a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Days to taproot"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ActivationGap" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ActivationGap> ;
    skos:definition "Taproot took effect 4,698 days after the genesis block of 3 January 2009."@en .

pg:S_Aggregation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Aggregating keys and signatures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Aggregation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Aggregation> ;
    skos:definition "This group covers what linearity allows: batch verification, the scriptless multisignature, the key cancellation attack against it and MuSig2, the standard that defends against it (Nick et al., 2022)."@en .

pg:S_AmountCommitment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Commitment to the amount (BIP143)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AmountCommitment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_AmountCommitment> ;
    skos:definition "In BIP143 the signature hash includes the amount of the coin being spent, so a signer cannot be tricked about the amount, which the legacy hash does not offer."@en .

pg:S_AnyTwoOfThree a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Any two of three shares"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AnyTwoOfThree" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_AnyTwoOfThree> ;
    skos:definition "Any two of the three shares give the same secret, here the number 31337."@en .

pg:S_AnyoneCanPayEffect a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Effect of ANYONECANPAY"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AnyoneCanPayEffect" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_AnyoneCanPayEffect> ;
    skos:definition "With ANYONECANPAY a signature stays valid when the other inputs are replaced, which is what lets strangers add inputs to a crowdfunding transaction."@en .

pg:S_AnyprevoutValues a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "ANYPREVOUT values (BIP118)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AnyprevoutValues" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_AnyprevoutValues> ;
    skos:definition "The proposal BIP118 would add the flags SIGHASH_ANYPREVOUT = 0x40 and SIGHASH_ANYPREVOUTANYSCRIPT = 0xc0 to tapscript, and it is still only a draft."@en .

pg:S_BatchVerification a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Batch verification"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BatchVerification" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_BatchVerification> ;
    skos:definition "Because the equation is linear, many signatures can be verified together as one random combination of their equations, and one bad signature in the batch makes the combined check fail."@en .

pg:S_BinaryChecksum a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Checksum of the release"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BinaryChecksum" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_BinaryChecksum> ;
    skos:definition "The Linux archive of Bitcoin Core 31.1 used for the run has the SHA-256 digest listed in the project's SHA256SUMS file."@en .

pg:S_Bip340Format a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The BIP340 format"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip340Format" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Bip340Format> ;
    skos:definition "This group covers the details that make the algorithm a standard: the test vectors, the 64-byte and 65-byte forms, the x-only keys, the tagged hashes, the default nonce and the key prefixing (Wuille et al., 2020)."@en .

pg:S_Bip340Vectors a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIP340 test vectors"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Bip340Vectors" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Bip340Vectors> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's BIP340 implementation matches all 19 vectors of the BIP, verifying 19 and reproducing the signature of each of the 8 vectors that give a secret key."@en .

pg:S_BipStatus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Status of the BIPs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BipStatus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_BipStatus> ;
    skos:definition "In the BIP index, 66, 143, 327, 340, 341 and 342 are Deployed, 146 is Closed, 373 and 54 are Complete, and 118, 390, 461 and 361 are Drafts."@en .

pg:S_BookSampleOnChain a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The book's sample on the chain"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BookSampleOnChain" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_BookSampleOnChain> ;
    skos:definition "The signature in the book's DER section is the real signature of a main-chain transaction mined on 27 December 2013."@en .

pg:S_BookSampleVerifies a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The sample verifies"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BookSampleVerifies" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_BookSampleVerifies> ;
    skos:definition "The book's sample verifies against the legacy signature hash of its transaction and the 65-byte public key in the same input, and fails if the hash type is changed."@en .

pg:S_BookTransactionTime a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Time of the book's transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BookTransactionTime" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_BookTransactionTime> ;
    skos:definition "The transaction whose signature the book prints was mined on 27 December 2013 at 23:11:54 UTC."@en .

pg:S_ChallengeOrder a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Order of commitment and challenge"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChallengeOrder" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ChallengeOrder> ;
    skos:definition "A prover who learns the challenge before committing can pass without the private key, which is why the commitment must come first."@en .

pg:S_CommitmentMatrix a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Matrix of commitments"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CommitmentMatrix" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CommitmentMatrix> ;
    skos:definition "A matrix of five probes shows what each flag commits to: changing a field that the flag covers changes the hash of the signed input, changing one it does not cover leaves it unchanged."@en .

pg:S_Commitments a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "What each flag commits to"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Commitments" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Commitments> ;
    skos:definition "This group measures what each flag commits to, by changing one field of a transaction at a time and by testing the signatures that Bitcoin Core made, and it shows the role of ANYONECANPAY (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ConsensusAndPolicy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Consensus rules and relay policy"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ConsensusAndPolicy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ConsensusAndPolicy> ;
    skos:definition "This group separates what Bitcoin Core refuses to relay from what makes a block invalid, using the verdicts of the node on altered signatures and the limit on legacy signature operations (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_Core30Sigops a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Release note of Core 30.0"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Core30Sigops" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Core30Sigops> ;
    skos:definition "The release notes of Bitcoin Core 30.0 state the limit of 2,500 legacy signature operations and name BIP54 as the reason."@en .

pg:S_CoreBipList a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "BIPs listed by Bitcoin Core"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreBipList" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreBipList> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's list of implemented BIPs has 66, 143, 327, 340, 373 and 390 and does not have the drafts 118, 461 and 361 or BIP 54."@en .

pg:S_CoreConstants a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Constants in Bitcoin Core"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreConstants" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreConstants> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's header declares SIGHASH_ALL, NONE, SINGLE and ANYONECANPAY as 1, 2, 3 and 0x80 and adds SIGHASH_DEFAULT = 0 for taproot only."@en .

pg:S_CoreDeterminism a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core signs deterministically"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreDeterminism" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreDeterminism> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 gives the same signature when it signs the same transaction twice, for ECDSA and for taproot, because it derives the nonce deterministically and uses zero auxiliary data for schnorr."@en .

pg:S_CoreFlagBytes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Flag bytes in Core's signatures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreFlagBytes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreFlagBytes> ;
    skos:definition "The byte at the end of a signature Core made is the flag that was requested, and a taproot signature made with the default flag has 64 bytes and no flag byte."@en .

pg:S_CoreMusigSupport a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "musig() in Bitcoin Core"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreMusigSupport" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreMusigSupport> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 30.0 added the musig() descriptor and the MuSig2 fields of PSBT, 31.0 added signing, and the descriptor documentation of 29.0 does not have it."@en .

pg:S_CoreNodeRun a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The node run"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreNodeRun" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreNodeRun> ;
    skos:definition "The signatures and verdicts of this chapter come from one run of Bitcoin Core 31.1.0 (version number 310100) on a private regtest network."@en .

pg:S_CoreRefusesSingleBug a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core refuses the SINGLE bug"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreRefusesSingleBug" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreRefusesSingleBug> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 leaves such an input unsigned instead of creating the signature of the number one."@en .

pg:S_CoreSignatureSurvival a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Survival of Core's signatures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreSignatureSurvival" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreSignatureSurvival> ;
    skos:definition "Signatures made by Bitcoin Core 31.1 with each flag survive exactly the changes the flag allows: NONE survives any change of outputs and SINGLE only a change of a different output."@en .

pg:S_CoreSignaturesVerify a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core's signatures verify"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoreSignaturesVerify" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_CoreSignaturesVerify> ;
    skos:definition "Every one of the 75 signatures in the 25 transactions that Bitcoin Core 31.1 signed verifies with the chapter's own implementation of all four signature hash algorithms."@en .

pg:S_DefaultNonce a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The default BIP340 nonce"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DefaultNonce" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DefaultNonce> ;
    skos:definition "The default BIP340 signing function derives its nonce from the key, the message and auxiliary data, so with the same inputs it gives the same signature and with other auxiliary data another one."@en .

pg:S_DerEncoding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "DER serialization"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DerEncoding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DerEncoding> ;
    skos:definition "This group takes the book's DER example apart, shows that it is a real signature from December 2013, verifies it, and notes its high R value (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_DerLayout a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Layout of a DER signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DerLayout" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DerLayout> ;
    skos:definition "A DER-encoded ECDSA signature is a sequence of two integers, tagged 0x30, with a length byte, followed in Bitcoin by the hash type byte; the book's sample has nine elements."@en .

pg:S_DerPadding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The padding zero of DER"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DerPadding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DerPadding> ;
    skos:definition "A DER integer is signed, so an R whose first byte is 0x88 or higher needs a leading zero byte, which makes the book's signature 71 bytes before the hash type byte and 72 with it."@en .

pg:S_DeterministicNonces a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Deterministic nonces"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DeterministicNonces" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DeterministicNonces> ;
    skos:definition "This group covers the cure, deriving the nonce from the key and the message: RFC 6979, the chapter's signer, and what Bitcoin Core does (Pornin, 2013)."@en .

pg:S_DeterministicSigning a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Deterministic signing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DeterministicSigning" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DeterministicSigning> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's ECDSA signer is deterministic: the same key and message give the same signature, another message gives another."@en .

pg:S_DigestCommitment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The signed digest"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DigestCommitment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DigestCommitment> ;
    skos:definition "What a signature signs is a hash computed from selected fields of the transaction, so changing a committed field changes the hash and breaks the signature."@en .

pg:S_DistinctNonces a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Distinct nonces"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DistinctNonces" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_DistinctNonces> ;
    skos:definition "With two different nonces the same formula returns a number that is not the key, so the attack needs the repetition."@en .

pg:S_EcdsaFormulas a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "ECDSA formulas"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EcdsaFormulas" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_EcdsaFormulas> ;
    skos:definition "ECDSA signs with a random nonce k, takes R as the x coordinate of kG and computes s = k^-1 (hash + x R) modulo n; the verifier recomputes R from s, the hash and the public key."@en .

pg:S_EcdsaMath a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The ECDSA equations"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EcdsaMath" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_EcdsaMath> ;
    skos:definition "This group gives the ECDSA equations, the malleability that follows from them and the low-S rule that removes it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_EcdsaSignatures a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "ECDSA signatures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EcdsaSignatures" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_EcdsaSignatures> ;
    skos:definition "ECDSA is the signature algorithm that Bitcoin used from its first release and that it still uses for every output that is not a taproot output."@en .

pg:S_EvidenceTrail a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The evidence trail"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EvidenceTrail" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_EvidenceTrail> ;
    skos:definition "This group describes how the package's evidence was gathered and kept: the sources, the node run and the release checksum (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FiatShamirIntegers a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "An identity round with small numbers"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FiatShamirIntegers" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_FiatShamirIntegers> ;
    skos:definition "With the response s = k + e x, the verifier's check s g = r + e X holds, and with a different response it would fail."@en .

pg:S_FiatShamirSignature a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The Fiat-Shamir signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FiatShamirSignature" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_FiatShamirSignature> ;
    skos:definition "The Fiat-Shamir transform replaces the verifier's random challenge by a hash of the commitment, the key and the message, which turns the interactive proof into a signature on that message."@en .

pg:S_FirstTaprootSpend a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A first taproot spend"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FirstTaprootSpend" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_FirstTaprootSpend> ;
    skos:definition "The taproot key-path spend recorded as the first on the main chain has a one-element witness holding a 64-byte schnorr signature, which verifies with the chapter's code."@en .

pg:S_FlagByte a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The flag byte by hand"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FlagByte" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_FlagByte> ;
    skos:definition "The flag byte is the base value 1, 2 or 3 combined by bitwise OR with 0x80 for ANYONECANPAY, so 0x83 means SINGLE together with ANYONECANPAY."@en .

pg:S_FlagCoding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "How flags are coded"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FlagCoding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_FlagCoding> ;
    skos:definition "This group gives the numbers of the flags: the six combinations of ECDSA, the extra default value of taproot, the seven valid taproot hash types and the proposed ANYPREVOUT values, each read from a standard or from Bitcoin Core's header file (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_FlagValues a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Flag values"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FlagValues" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_FlagValues> ;
    skos:definition "The three base flags are the numbers 1, 2 and 3 and ANYONECANPAY adds 0x80, which gives the six combinations 1, 2, 3, 129, 130 and 131."@en .

pg:S_GrindingSource a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Grinding in the source"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GrindingSource" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_GrindingSource> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's key.cpp has the low-R grind and the RFC 6979 function, and sign.cpp has the zero auxiliary value and the refusal to produce the SINGLE bug signature."@en .

pg:S_GroupArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Arithmetic of the curve"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GroupArithmetic" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_GroupArithmetic> ;
    skos:definition "This group concerns the numbers that signature equations use, in particular the two different moduli of the curve and the round trip of signing and verifying, which the chapter's plain-Python implementation passes before any comparison with other software (Wuille et al., 2020)."@en .

pg:S_HashedBytesGrowth a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bytes hashed grow with inputs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashedBytesGrowth" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_HashedBytesGrowth> ;
    skos:definition "If each of n signatures hashes about the whole transaction, and the transaction grows with n, the bytes hashed grow with n squared: 20 inputs cost about 3.9 times what 10 inputs cost."@en .

pg:S_HashingCost a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Cost of hashing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashingCost" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_HashingCost> ;
    skos:definition "This group counts the bytes hashed by the legacy and BIP143 algorithms to show the quadratic and the linear growth (Lau and Wuille, 2016)."@en .

pg:S_HashingGrowth a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Growth of hashing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HashingGrowth" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_HashingGrowth> ;
    skos:definition "Going from 100 to 1,000 inputs multiplies the legacy work by 97.8 and the segwit work by 10.0."@en .

pg:S_History a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "History and dates"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#History" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_History> ;
    skos:definition "This group gives the dates behind the chapter's history, the patent, the book's real transaction and the taproot activation, each computed or read from a saved source (Cornell Legal Information Institute, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_IdentityProtocol a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "From identity proof to signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#IdentityProtocol" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_IdentityProtocol> ;
    skos:definition "This group builds the schnorr signature step by step: the toy protocol with integers, the identification round with points on the curve, what goes wrong if the order of the moves is changed, and the Fiat-Shamir step that makes it a signature (Fiat and Shamir, 1986)."@en .

pg:S_IdentityRound a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "One round of the identity proof"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#IdentityRound" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_IdentityRound> ;
    skos:definition "On the real curve the same three moves (commitment kG, challenge e, response s) are checked by one equation, sG = kG + eX, and it holds."@en .

pg:S_InputIndependence a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Independent signatures per input"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#InputIndependence" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_InputIndependence> ;
    skos:definition "Each input of a transaction is signed separately, so owners of different inputs can sign the same transaction one after the other and neither signature can be moved to the other input."@en .

pg:S_KeyBinding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Binding to the key"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KeyBinding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_KeyBinding> ;
    skos:definition "A signature verifies only against the public key that matches the private key that made it, which is what ties a spend to the owner of the coins."@en .

pg:S_KeyCancellation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Key cancellation attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KeyCancellation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_KeyCancellation> ;
    skos:definition "In the naive protocol the last signer to publish a key can choose it as their key minus the sum of the others, and then control the aggregate alone."@en .

pg:S_KeyCancellationToy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Key cancellation with small numbers"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KeyCancellationToy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_KeyCancellationToy> ;
    skos:definition "If Bob announces his key as m g minus Alice's key, the sum of the two keys is m g, a key that Bob alone controls."@en .

pg:S_KeyPrefixing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Key prefixing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KeyPrefixing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_KeyPrefixing> ;
    skos:definition "Including the public key in the challenge stops an attacker who knows a signature for one key from deriving a signature for a related key such as a BIP32 child key."@en .

pg:S_KnownPitfalls a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Known pitfalls of the flags"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#KnownPitfalls" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_KnownPitfalls> ;
    skos:definition "This group collects a flaw the book does not mention, the SIGHASH_SINGLE bug, how Bitcoin Core reacts to it, and the test vectors of Bitcoin Core that guard the legacy hash against such errors (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_LegacySigopsCap a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Cap on legacy signature operations"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LegacySigopsCap" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LegacySigopsCap> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 30.0 allows at most 2,500 potentially executed legacy signature operations in a standard transaction, the limit that BIP54 would make a consensus rule."@en .

pg:S_LibraryHighR a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "High R without grinding"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LibraryHighR" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LibraryHighR> ;
    skos:definition "A signer that does not grind produces a high R in about half of its signatures: of 400 signatures made by the chapter's library, 197 had the top bit of R set."@en .

pg:S_LibraryNonceMatches a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Library nonce matches the standard"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LibraryNonceMatches" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LibraryNonceMatches> ;
    skos:definition "The signature the chapter's signer makes is the one obtained from RFC 6979's nonce and the plain ECDSA formulas, normalized to low s."@en .

pg:S_LibrarySupport a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Library and release support"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LibrarySupport" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LibrarySupport> ;
    skos:definition "This group records changes in the library and the release that implements the signatures, which are the places where the book's text has aged (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_LibsecpChanges a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Changes in libsecp256k1"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LibsecpChanges" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LibsecpChanges> ;
    skos:definition "The secp256k1 library removed its deprecated function alias for schnorr signing in version 0.8.0, and Bitcoin Core calls the function with the name secp256k1_schnorrsig_sign32."@en .

pg:S_Linearity a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Linearity"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Linearity" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Linearity> ;
    skos:definition "Multiplying the generator by a scalar is additive and homogeneous, which is why signatures and keys of several signers can be added."@en .

pg:S_LowRGrinding a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Low-R grinding"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LowRGrinding" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LowRGrinding> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core signs repeatedly until R is small, so all 300 signatures in a transaction with 300 inputs are 70 or 71 bytes with the hash type and none is longer."@en .

pg:S_LowRTries a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chance of a low R"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LowRTries" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LowRTries> ;
    skos:definition "Because a low R is a first byte below 0x80, each attempt succeeds with a chance of one half, so 8 attempts succeed with a chance of 0.9961 and at most 256 values of the counter need to be considered."@en .

pg:S_LowSRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The low-S rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LowSRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_LowSRule> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's signer always returns the low s, so every signature it makes passes the strict DER and low-S rules, with lengths 70 or 71 bytes."@en .

pg:S_ManipulationAttack a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Manipulation attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ManipulationAttack" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ManipulationAttack> ;
    skos:definition "In the book's simple threshold protocol a dishonest member can obtain the others' partial keys by claiming in turn that each of the others is unavailable."@en .

pg:S_MessageIntegrity a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Integrity of the message"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MessageIntegrity" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_MessageIntegrity> ;
    skos:definition "A signature covers its message, so changing even one bit of the message makes the signature fail."@en .

pg:S_ModularRemainder a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Remainder of a division"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ModularRemainder" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ModularRemainder> ;
    skos:definition "Reducing a number modulo m keeps only the remainder of its division by m, so 5 modulo 3 is 2, which is the book's own example of what modulus means."@en .

pg:S_MuSigBlocksCancellation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "MuSig2 against key cancellation"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MuSigBlocksCancellation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_MuSigBlocksCancellation> ;
    skos:definition "With BIP327's coefficients the aggregate of Alice's key and Bob's cancelling key is no longer Bob's key, so Bob cannot sign alone."@en .

pg:S_MuSigKeyAgg a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "MuSig2 key aggregation"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MuSigKeyAgg" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_MuSigKeyAgg> ;
    skos:definition "The MuSig2 key aggregation of BIP327 weights each key by a coefficient derived from all the keys, and the chapter's implementation reproduces the BIP's test vectors."@en .

pg:S_NaiveMultisig a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Naive scriptless multisignature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NaiveMultisig" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_NaiveMultisig> ;
    skos:definition "Two signers can add their public keys and their signatures into one key and one signature that verifies, which looks like the signature of a single person."@en .

pg:S_NonceReuse a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Nonce reuse"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonceReuse" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_NonceReuse> ;
    skos:definition "If two ECDSA signatures from the same key use the same nonce k, anyone can compute k and then the private key from the two signatures."@en .

pg:S_NonceReuseRecovery a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Recovering a key from a reused nonce"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonceReuseRecovery" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_NonceReuseRecovery> ;
    skos:definition "Two ECDSA signatures that use the same nonce k give k as the difference of the digests divided by the difference of the signature values, and then the private key."@en .

pg:S_NonceSafety a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Safety of the nonce"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NonceSafety" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_NonceSafety> ;
    skos:definition "This group shows the consequence of repeating the nonce, the recovery of the private key from two signatures, and that the recovery needs the repetition (Breitner and Heninger, 2019)."@en .

pg:S_OtherValidS a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The other valid S"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OtherValidS" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_OtherValidS> ;
    skos:definition "If s is a valid ECDSA value then n - s is valid too, and exactly one of the two lies in the lower half of the range that BIP146 asks for."@en .

pg:S_PatentDates a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Patent dates"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PatentDates" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_PatentDates> ;
    skos:definition "Schnorr's patent 4,995,082 was filed on 23 February 1990 and published on 19 February 1991, so the statute's greater term ends on 23 February 2010."@en .

pg:S_PatentTermYears a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The patent term in years"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PatentTermYears" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_PatentTermYears> ;
    skos:definition "A patent filed in 1990 and granted in 1991 ran to the greater of 20 years from filing, 2010, and 17 years from grant, 2008, which is 2010."@en .

pg:S_PolicyFlags a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Policy flags"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PolicyFlags" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_PolicyFlags> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's standard policy flags include the low-S, null-fail, strict encoding and DER signature checks."@en .

pg:S_PolicyVerdicts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core's policy verdicts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PolicyVerdicts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_PolicyVerdicts> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepts a normal signature into its mempool and rejects a high-S signature, a non-minimal DER encoding and a signature of another message with a specific error each."@en .

pg:S_QuadraticHashing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Quadratic hashing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#QuadraticHashing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_QuadraticHashing> ;
    skos:definition "In the legacy algorithm every signature check hashes nearly the whole transaction, so the bytes hashed grow with the square of the number of inputs; BIP143 hashes each part once."@en .

pg:S_Randomness a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Randomness and signature size"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Randomness" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Randomness> ;
    skos:definition "The nonce of a signature must be unpredictable to everyone and used once, because with a repeated or biased nonce the private key can be computed from the signatures; this branch shows the algebra of the recovery, the standard remedy of deriving the nonce deterministically from the key and the message (RFC 6979 for ECDSA and the default algorithm of BIP340 for schnorr), what Bitcoin Core does in practice (deterministic nonces, grinding for a small R, zero auxiliary data for taproot) and what that does to the size of signatures."@en .

pg:S_RetargetBoundary a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Activation on a retarget boundary"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RetargetBoundary" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_RetargetBoundary> ;
    skos:definition "Block 709,632 is exactly 352 retarget periods of 2,016 blocks after the genesis block, and the first taproot spend followed it by 3 blocks."@en .

pg:S_Rfc6979Vector a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "RFC 6979 test vector"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Rfc6979Vector" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_Rfc6979Vector> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's implementation of the nonce derivation of RFC 6979 reproduces the nonce printed in the RFC's appendix for the curve P-256."@en .

pg:S_SampleHighR a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The sample's high R"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SampleHighR" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SampleHighR> ;
    skos:definition "The sample's R has its top bit set, which costs a padding zero and one extra byte, while its s is the low form."@en .

pg:S_ScalarModulus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Scalars are reduced modulo n"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ScalarModulus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ScalarModulus> ;
    skos:definition "The numbers in a signature equation (nonce, challenge, private key, response) are reduced modulo the order n of the curve's group, not modulo the field prime p that the book names."@en .

pg:S_SchnorrSavings a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bytes saved by schnorr"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SchnorrSavings" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SchnorrSavings> ;
    skos:definition "A schnorr signature of 64 bytes with a 32-byte key replaces an ECDSA signature of 72 bytes with a 33-byte key, which saves 9 bytes of witness for each input and 27 for 3 inputs."@en .

pg:S_SchnorrSignatures a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Schnorr signatures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SchnorrSignatures" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SchnorrSignatures> ;
    skos:definition "Schnorr signatures, introduced to Bitcoin by the taproot soft fork of November 2021 as BIP340, are the newer of the two signature algorithms of the chapter and the one whose mathematics is simplest: a three-move proof of knowledge of a private key, turned into a signature by replacing the verifier's challenge with a hash."@en .

pg:S_SchnorrToTaprootYears a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Years from Schnorr to taproot"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SchnorrToTaprootYears" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SchnorrToTaprootYears> ;
    skos:definition "Thirty years separate the publication of Schnorr's journal paper in 1991 from the activation of schnorr signatures in Bitcoin in 2021, and 32 years the Crypto '89 priority."@en .

pg:S_SegwitSigning a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Segwit signing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SegwitSigning" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SegwitSigning> ;
    skos:definition "Segregated witness changed how the data that is signed is built, and this branch collects the two consequences that the chapter's last section and the later BIPs describe: the cost of verifying a transaction with many inputs, which grew with the square of the number of inputs in the old algorithm and grows linearly in the BIP143 algorithm, and the commitment of the signature to the amount of the coin being spent (and, in BIP341, to all the amounts), which the old algorithm did not give."@en .

pg:S_ShamirModulus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Shamir sharing modulo 101"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ShamirModulus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ShamirModulus> ;
    skos:definition "A secret of 7 and a slope of 3 give the shares 10, 13 and 16 at x = 1, 2 and 3 modulo 101, and the first and third shares return the secret 7."@en .

pg:S_ShamirSharing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Shamir secret sharing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ShamirSharing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ShamirSharing> ;
    skos:definition "Shamir's secret sharing hides a secret as the constant term of a polynomial and gives each holder one point, so that any two of three points rebuild the secret by interpolation."@en .

pg:S_ShareSecrecy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Secrecy of a single share"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ShareSecrecy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ShareSecrecy> ;
    skos:definition "A single share of a 2-of-3 sharing reveals nothing, because for any other secret there is a line through the same point."@en .

pg:S_SighashFlags a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signature hash flags (SIGHASH)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SighashFlags" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SighashFlags> ;
    skos:definition "A signature does not have to cover the whole transaction: the SIGHASH flag, one byte at the end of the signature, selects which inputs and outputs the signed hash commits to, and this lets several people build one transaction together, for example a crowdfunding payment to which strangers add inputs."@en .

pg:S_SighashVectors a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core's sighash test vectors"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SighashVectors" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SighashVectors> ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's legacy signature hash agrees with Bitcoin Core's own test file for all 500 vectors, which cover eight kinds of hash types."@en .

pg:S_SignRoundtrip a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sign and verify round trip"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignRoundtrip" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SignRoundtrip> ;
    skos:definition "Signing with a private key and verifying with the public key returns yes, the basic round trip every signature scheme must satisfy."@en .

pg:S_SignatureFoundations a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Foundations of digital signatures"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignatureFoundations" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SignatureFoundations> ;
    skos:definition "A digital signature is the proof that the person who controls a private key has authorized a transaction, and this branch collects what the chapter's first sections say about it: the three jobs of a signature (authorization by the owner, non-repudiation and integrity of the signed data), the numbers it is computed with, and the fact that each input of a transaction carries its own independent signature."@en .

pg:S_SignatureMalleability a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signature malleability"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignatureMalleability" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SignatureMalleability> ;
    skos:definition "For every valid ECDSA signature (R, s) the pair (R, n - s) is also valid for the same message, so a third party can change a signature without knowing the key."@en .

pg:S_SignatureRoles a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "What a signature does"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignatureRoles" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SignatureRoles> ;
    skos:definition "This group states what a signature verification needs and promises: three inputs, the answer yes only if they belong together, and the three purposes the book names, which are authorization, non-repudiation and integrity (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_SignatureSize a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signature size and nonce grinding"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignatureSize" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SignatureSize> ;
    skos:definition "This group covers the effect of the nonce on the length of ECDSA signatures and the grinding that Bitcoin Core does to keep them short (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_SignatureSizes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Schnorr signature sizes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignatureSizes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SignatureSizes> ;
    skos:definition "A BIP340 signature has 64 bytes, the 32-byte x coordinate of the nonce point followed by the 32-byte scalar s, and in taproot one more byte may name a hash type."@en .

pg:S_SigopCapInputs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Inputs under the operation cap"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SigopCapInputs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SigopCapInputs> ;
    skos:definition "If every input of a standard transaction executes 2 legacy signature operations, the cap of 2,500 allows at most 1,250 such inputs."@en .

pg:S_SinceTheBook a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Since the book went to print"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SinceTheBook" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SinceTheBook> ;
    skos:definition "The 3rd edition went to print in 2023 and some of its statements have moved: proposals have changed status, Bitcoin Core has added support for MuSig2 and a limit on signature operations, a threshold signature BIP has been proposed, the library has changed an interface, and two new drafts concern ECDSA and the quantum threat."@en .

pg:S_SingleBug a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The SIGHASH_SINGLE bug"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SingleBug" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SingleBug> ;
    skos:definition "In the legacy algorithm, SIGHASH_SINGLE on an input that has no output of the same index does not sign a hash of the transaction but the number one."@en .

pg:S_SingleBugIndexes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Inputs without an output"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SingleBugIndexes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SingleBugIndexes> ;
    skos:definition "With SIGHASH_SINGLE an input whose index is not smaller than the number of outputs has no output to sign, which is the case that the legacy algorithm answers with the number one."@en .

pg:S_SlowSigops a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Twenty-five seconds for 5,569 operations"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SlowSigops" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SlowSigops> ;
    skos:definition "BIP143 reports that a block of 1 MB takes about 2 seconds to verify but a 1 MB transaction with 5,569 signature operations may take 25 seconds, about 4.5 milliseconds for each operation."@en .

pg:S_SourcesRecord a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Record of the sources"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SourcesRecord" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_SourcesRecord> ;
    skos:definition "All 66 sources of this package answered with HTTP 200 and each passage the text relies on was found in the saved copy."@en .

pg:S_StandardsStatus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Status of the standards"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StandardsStatus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_StandardsStatus> ;
    skos:definition "This group states the present status of the proposals and the support in Bitcoin Core that the book describes as future or does not mention (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_StrictDerConsensus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Strict DER is consensus"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#StrictDerConsensus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_StrictDerConsensus> ;
    skos:definition "A high-S signature is valid in a block, because low-S is only policy, while a signature that is not strict DER makes the block invalid, because strict DER is a consensus rule."@en .

pg:S_TaggedHash a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tagged hashes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaggedHash" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TaggedHash> ;
    skos:definition "BIP340 hashes with a tag: the hash of the tag is computed once and put twice in front of the data, so that hashes made for different purposes cannot be confused."@en .

pg:S_TaggedPrefixBlock a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The tag prefix fills a block"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaggedPrefixBlock" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TaggedPrefixBlock> ;
    skos:definition "BIP340 prefixes the data with the hash of the tag twice, which is 64 bytes and exactly one block of SHA-256, so an implementation can precompute it."@en .

pg:S_TaprootAmounts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Commitment to all amounts (BIP341)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaprootAmounts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TaprootAmounts> ;
    skos:definition "A taproot signature commits to the amounts of all inputs being spent, unless ANYONECANPAY is used, when it commits only to its own."@en .

pg:S_TaprootHashTypes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Valid taproot hash types"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaprootHashTypes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TaprootHashTypes> ;
    skos:definition "A taproot signature may use only the hash types 0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x81, 0x82 and 0x83, and every other byte value makes the signature invalid."@en .

pg:S_TaprootSigning a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Taproot signing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaprootSigning" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TaprootSigning> ;
    skos:definition "This group checks a real taproot signature from the main chain and the verdicts of Bitcoin Core on altered ones (Wuille et al., 2020)."@en .

pg:S_TaprootVerdicts a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Core's taproot verdicts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaprootVerdicts" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TaprootVerdicts> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepts the 64-byte default and the explicit ALL signature and rejects an explicit zero hash type, a 63-byte signature and two altered signatures, each with its own message."@en .

pg:S_ThresholdBipGap a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "No threshold BIP"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ThresholdBipGap" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ThresholdBipGap> ;
    skos:definition "The BIP index has no entry for a threshold signature scheme, while Optech lists a 2024 mention of a BIP proposed for FROST."@en .

pg:S_ThresholdSharing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Threshold signatures from secret sharing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ThresholdSharing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ThresholdSharing> ;
    skos:definition "This group covers the threshold signatures that the book builds from Shamir's secret sharing, any two of three signers, why one share reveals nothing and the manipulation attack that the book warns about (Shamir, 1979)."@en .

pg:S_ToyEcdsaCheck a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "ECDSA with small numbers"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ToyEcdsaCheck" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ToyEcdsaCheck> ;
    skos:definition "With a stand-in group of integers modulo 101, the ECDSA equation s = k^-1 (z + r d) gives a signature that the verifier's combination of z/s and r/s accepts."@en .

pg:S_ToyProtocol a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The toy protocol with integers"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ToyProtocol" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ToyProtocol> ;
    skos:definition "With ordinary integers the identification protocol works, s = k + e x satisfies s G = k G + e (x G), but anybody can divide by G, so the private key is exposed."@en .

pg:S_TransactionSigning a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signing a transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TransactionSigning" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_TransactionSigning> ;
    skos:definition "This group concerns what is signed in a transaction: a digest computed from selected fields, signed separately for every input, so that different owners can contribute their own signatures to one transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ValueCommitment a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Commitment to amounts"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ValueCommitment" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_ValueCommitment> ;
    skos:definition "This group shows the commitment of signatures to the value of the coins, which BIP143 introduced and BIP341 extended (Wuille et al., 2020)."@en .

pg:S_VerifierInputs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Inputs of verification"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#VerifierInputs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_VerifierInputs> ;
    skos:definition "A verifier needs exactly three inputs, the message, the public key and the signature, and the answer is yes only when all three belong together."@en .

pg:S_WrongResponse a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A wrong response"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WrongResponse" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_WrongResponse> ;
    skos:definition "If the response s is changed by even one unit, the verification equation no longer holds, so a prover without the private key cannot answer."@en .

pg:S_XOnlyKey a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "X-only public keys"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#XOnlyKey" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08#S_XOnlyKey> ;
    skos:definition "A BIP340 public key is only the 32-byte x coordinate, and the signer negates the private key when the point's y coordinate is odd so that a key and its negative give the same public key."@en .

pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Separate what consensus requires of a signature from what relay policy requires (strict DER, low S, taproot hash types), and name what has changed in the standards and in Bitcoin Core since the book went to print"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can separate what consensus requires of a signature from what relay policy requires (strict DER, low S, taproot hash types), and name what has changed in the standards and in Bitcoin Core since the book went to print."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Follow the step from an identity proof to a Fiat-Shamir signature and check a BIP340 signature: 64 bytes, an x-only key, a tagged hash and a default nonce"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can follow the step from an identity proof to a Fiat-Shamir signature and check a BIP340 signature: 64 bytes, an x-only key, a tagged hash and a default nonce."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Judge the safety of a signing nonce: reuse, deterministic nonces by RFC 6979, low-R grinding and the size of a DER signature, with the Android random-number failure as the case"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Evaluate ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can judge the safety of a signing nonce: reuse, deterministic nonces by RFC 6979, low-R grinding and the size of a DER signature, with the Android random-number failure as the case."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sign and verify a message with the ECDSA equations on the chapter's curve, say which three inputs verification needs, and explain why exponents are reduced modulo n while coordinates are reduced modulo p"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can sign and verify a message with the ECDSA equations on the chapter's curve, say which three inputs verification needs, and explain why exponents are reduced modulo n while coordinates are reduced modulo p."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Decide, for each signature hash flag with and without ANYONECANPAY, which parts of a transaction a signature commits to, and explain the SIGHASH_SINGLE bug and how Bitcoin Core refuses it"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch08/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can decide, for each signature hash flag with and without ANYONECANPAY, which parts of a transaction a signature commits to, and explain the SIGHASH_SINGLE bug and how Bitcoin Core refuses it."@en .

